w11 for Debian/Ubuntu
=============================

One package, both releases
--------------------------

The package is Architecture: all and every module it ships is pure Python
standard library, installed into the version-independent
/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages.  The same .deb therefore installs on Ubuntu
24.04 (Python 3.12) and 26.04 (Python 3.14); nothing in it is compiled against
one of them.  The .pyc files are made on your machine, by your python3, when
the package is configured.

What is installed where
-----------------------

  /usr/bin/{wdotool,wwmctl,wxprop,wxrandr,warandr,wmirror,xw11}
        the six tools -- seven commands, counting xw11, the X11 proxy the four
        wrappers start for themselves.  Under their own names only: no file of
        xdotool, wmctrl, x11-utils or x11-xserver-utils is touched or replaced,
        which is what keeps the X11 handover working.
  /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/{w11common,hacks,wdotool,wwmctl,wxprop,
                                 wxrandr,warandr,wmirror,xw11}/
        the code.
  /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/w11-bridge@w11/
        the GNOME Shell bridge extension, system-wide.
  /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/w11-overlap@w11/
        the second, separate GNOME Shell extension, the one behind
        `wxrandr --unsafe-gnome-overlap`.  Shipped so that it is there, and
        enabled for nobody: no postinst, no autostart helper and no user's
        dconf touches it, which is deliberate, because it is the only thing
        in this package that can cost you the session you are sitting in.
        Turning it on is `gnome-extensions enable
        w11-overlap@w11` and a re-login, by hand.  Even then
        nothing calls it until an apply carries
        `wxrandr --unsafe-gnome-overlap`, which prints what it is about to do
        in full every time until `wxrandr --gnome-overlap-allow` records an
        agreement -- that agreement changes what is printed and nothing else.
        See Overlapping monitors on GNOME in
        /usr/share/doc/w11/README.md.gz.
  /usr/lib/w11/enable-bridge
  /usr/lib/w11/enable-bridge.desktop
        the once-per-user helper that enables the extension (see below) and
        its autostart entry.  Both are packaging/common/enable-bridge and
        its .desktop in the source tree, not debian/: they were never
        Debian-specific, and all three packagings install the same two
        files (the rpm rewrites the Exec= path to /usr/libexec and nothing
        else touches them).  The autostart entry reaches /etc/xdg/autostart
        as a symlink:
        a regular file under /etc is a conffile, and a conffile outlives
        `apt remove` -- an autostart entry pointing at a helper the removal
        has just deleted.  The symlink goes when the package goes.
  /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-w11-uinput.rules
  /usr/lib/modules-load.d/w11-uinput.conf
        /dev/uinput for the user at the active seat.
  /usr/share/applications/warandr.desktop
        the menu entry for the GUI.

The bridge extension, and why a re-login is the one manual step
---------------------------------------------------------------

gnome-shell scans extension directories only when a session starts, and
whether an extension is enabled is a per-user dconf setting.  dpkg runs as
root, possibly with no user session on the machine at all, so it can do
neither of those things itself.  What the package does instead:

  * installs the extension where gnome-shell will find it at the next login
    (the system directory -- no user's home is written to);
  * ships an autostart entry that runs /usr/lib/w11/enable-bridge
    inside each user's first GNOME session after the install.  That script
    adds the uuid to org.gnome.shell enabled-extensions once, writes a stamp
    in ~/.config/w11, and then leaves the setting alone -- so if you
    later disable the extension, it stays disabled.

    Once per installation, that is: removing the package deletes the system
    stamp /var/lib/w11/installed, installing writes a fresh one, and
    the helper enables the extension again for any user whose own stamp is
    older than it.  Without that, a remove (which takes the extension with it,
    and with it the reason for the uuid to stay in anyone's dconf) followed by
    an install would leave the user with a stamp, no setting and no extension
    -- and the install's own message promising it had been enabled for them.
    An upgrade keeps the system stamp and so disturbs nobody's choice.

None of the above is about the overlap extension: the helper knows one uuid,
the bridge's, and the other one stays off until somebody enables it by hand.

So: install, log out, log back in, and the window commands work.  Before that
relogin wxrandr, warandr and wdotool's input commands already work, and the
window commands say the bridge is not running.

  gnome-extensions list --enabled | grep w11           # is it on for me?
  sh gnome/install-bridge.sh --check                   # the full report

If you had previously installed the extension per user with
gnome/install-bridge.sh (no --system), that copy lives in
~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions and shadows the packaged one.  Remove it
with `sh gnome/install-bridge.sh --uninstall` to use the package's copy.

/dev/uinput
-----------

The rule tags the node `uaccess`, so systemd-logind hands the user of the
active seat an ACL on it at every login; postinst applies it to the running
system as well, so no reboot is needed.  The node itself stays root:root 0600
-- no `input` group, no standing grant to anyone who is not at the seat.
Removing the package puts the node back to root:root 0600 with no ACL.

Anyone who can open /dev/uinput can type as you.  That is the trade these
tools exist to make, and it is spelled out in the threat model in
/usr/share/doc/w11/README.md.gz.  If it is the wrong trade on this
machine, remove the rule and run the input commands under sudo instead:

  sudo rm /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-w11-uinput.rules
  sudo udevadm control --reload-rules

Alongside a pip install from the clone
--------------------------------------

The package and a venv install of the same source live together: /usr/local/bin
comes before /usr/bin on Ubuntu's PATH, so the symlinks the README's pip route
makes keep winning for the six command names, dpkg owns nothing under
/usr/local, and `dpkg -V w11` stays clean either way.

One thing is worth knowing if the clone is what you are working on.  A venv made
with --system-site-packages sees /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages, and setuptools'
editable install (`pip install -e .`) works by appending a finder to
sys.meta_path -- after the ordinary path finder.  So once this package is
installed, `import wdotool` inside that venv resolves to the packaged copy, not
to your clone, and the venv's own commands run the packaged code.  Measured on
both releases: 24.04 with Python 3.12 and 26.04 with Python 3.14.  Either do
not install the package on the machine you develop on, or point at the clone
explicitly:

  PYTHONPATH=/path/to/the/clone ~/.venvs/w11/bin/wdotool ...

A non-editable install into the venv (`pip install .`) is not affected: those
files are in the venv's own site-packages, which comes first.

wmirror and wl-mirror
---------------------

wmirror drives wl-mirror, which is a Suggests and not a Depends: it is only
useful where the compositor offers a screencopy protocol, which the wlroots
family does and GNOME does not.  On GNOME `wmirror --check` says so in full --
"this compositor advertises neither zwlr_screencopy_manager_v1 nor
ext_image_copy_capture_manager_v1" -- with or without wl-mirror installed.

Installing over the originals
-----------------------------

Deliberately not done by the package.  /usr/local/bin comes first on Ubuntu's
PATH, so it is one symlink per tool and no packaged file is disturbed:

  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wdotool /usr/local/bin/xdotool
  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wwmctl  /usr/local/bin/wmctrl
  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wxprop  /usr/local/bin/xprop
  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wxrandr /usr/local/bin/xrandr

The other two packagings
------------------------

The same tree builds a Fedora rpm and an Arch package, and each has a file of
this one's shape beside its recipe: packaging/rpm/README.Fedora and
packaging/arch/README.Arch.  Three things differ from the .deb and each is a
decision rather than an accident: the rpm is three packages where this is one
(the two extensions are subpackages, and the overlap one has no Supplements,
so nothing installs it for you); the rpm's GTK stack is Recommends rather
than a hard dependency; and neither of the two is committed to the tree,
because both land in a version-pinned site-packages while this .deb is
Architecture: all and installs on both supported Ubuntu releases from one
file.  The udev procedure is the same procedure in three dialects, and the
tests compare postinst/postrm, %post/%postun and post_install/post_remove
phrase for phrase, so a change to one turns the other two red.

